# Our Gita!
Tamil title: நமது கீதை!

“I heard something,” said Ganapathi Subramania Iyer, wetting in the water the dhoti he had brought.

“What is it, man? You always drag something in and start talking,” replied Sivasami Iyer irritably as he scrubbed a copper vessel.

“Will you believe me if I tell you? You know the Japanese emperor, Hirohito? Last week he performed a *shraddha*. Did you know?”

“Enough. Shut your mouth. Kanaka may hear you. Her husband is this town's sub-inspector. If she reports that these people were discussing Japan, danger will come to you.”

“Why are you frightening me? Will Kanaka go and tell him? Even if she does, do I not know what he can do? Leave the joke aside and listen. This truly happened. The Japanese emperor performed a *shraddha*!” Ganapathi Iyer began again.

“I suppose Agni Bhagavan came and told you,” Sivasami mocked.

“Must you always joke? Listen. Last week he reportedly went to a place called Kyoto. There he worshipped his grandfathers, great-grandfathers and ancestral spirits and told them, ‘The Japanese will win this year.’ The news went from Tokyo to Berlin. The Germans announced it on their radio. The Hindu's reporter in London heard it and sent it to *The Hindu*. My daughter-in-law was reading it yesterday and told me. I was astonished.”

Sivasami Iyer too expressed surprise. “Is that really so? How astonishing! They say the Japanese are Buddhists. If they have *shraddha*, that is very good.”

Ganapathi Iyer said, “My nephew was saying the same yesterday. Even if the Japanese come here, they will apparently leave *shraddha* service to our people. He is a Congressman, you know. It seems there are some kind of talks between Congress and Japan.”

“You speak without thinking. Suppose the Japanese do arrange for our people to perform the rite. Will they give us vegetables as these ‘Shudras’ do? Is there anything those Japanese will not eat? Lizards, chameleons, snakes, scorpions, moss, frogs—they supposedly spare nothing. What shall we do if they invite us for *shraddha* and serve such horrors?”

Ganapathi Iyer's stomach turned merely hearing it. “Do those sinners even eat snakes?”

“Oh, you seem to know nothing. Not only snakes. Do you know what their food is like? You will vomit if you hear. They buy baskets of fish, dig a pit, pour the fish into it and cover it. The fish rot inside until they become slime and fill with worms.”

“Enough! Hearing it makes me retch. I had heard of such things in Burma. Do these people do it too?” Ganapathi interrupted.

Sivasami continued gleefully: “Listen to the rest. A little worm eats the larger one, grows, dies and is eaten by another, until in the end one enormous worm writhes in the pit. They dig it out, slice it like a ridge gourd, fry it in ghee and eat it. That is the food of the Chinese and Japanese.”

“What a curse! They eat worms? Karma, karma! And these people have *shraddha* too?” Ganapathi said, leaving the pond bank.

Sivasami followed him. “Do not dismiss them altogether. They may eat worms and insects as their national custom, but when they observe *shraddha*, it means they support our Aryan religion.”

“That is right. Yes, in one sense it is the Aryan path. I must ask my nephew,” Ganapathi replied before going home.

I asked Veeran, “Which town's pond-bank conversation is this?”

“That is exactly how that crowd will talk,” he answered.

Japan too possesses a superstition of this kind. It believes ancestral spirits move above and has a rite in which people of this world communicate with and serve them. The Japanese emperor performed this ceremony accordingly.

Hearing of it, groups of ritual priests at pond banks will rub their hands in delight and say: “The Japanese emperor follows our custom! We call it *shraddha*; that is precisely what he performed.” They may even imagine that when the Japanese arrive, they will receive opportunities to conduct the rites.

This cannot be dismissed merely as Veeran's joke. The Aryan leader Achariyar himself says that wherever any one of “our principles” appears, it must be regarded as our own. On that authority, Veeran says the Japanese emperor's superstitious rite will delight the Aryans.

That is the Aryan custom. Whatever exists anywhere, if it offers something sticky enough to grasp, they claim it as “ours,” establish a proprietary right and profit from it.

Consider dance. When “Shudra women” performed it, Aryans said: “How dreadful! Look at her gestures, rolling eyes and bent legs. Young men who watch will be corrupted.” When the same “art” is performed by Aryan maidens, they exclaim: “Ah, splendid dance! The *bhava* is first-rate. Is not dance a most excellent art? The Lord dancing with the gopis and Nataraja's cosmic dance reveal its very life.” Have you not seen them speak and swell with pride in this way?

The All-India Arya Dharma Seva Sangh—please do not forget the name—reportedly celebrated an anniversary. Achariyar sent this benediction:

“Your duty is to remain in service. One must always labour for the welfare of humankind and not seek personal benefit. These are the secret of the Gita. Whatever religion teaches these truths, they are the philosophy of the Gita. Its name and method may be anything; that does not matter.”

Achariyar thus declares it Arya Dharma to claim as one's own whatever resembles or contains the essence of one's “principle.” By that same method, the pond-bank priests rejoice that the Japanese emperor's communication with ancestral spirits is Aryan.

The thought Achariyar extracts from the Gita is a universal ethic and therefore will naturally occur in every path. But the philosophies contained in a religion are one thing; the practices people adopt as religion are another; and the arrogance with which religious gurus exploit those people is yet another.

The Gita's teaching is one thing, the storyteller's life another, and the condition of people who hear the tale, worship the ritual priest and confuse their minds yet another.

The story supposedly says, “Your duty is to remain at work.” Very well. What work do the Aryan holy Brahmins who are authors of Gita sermons perform? Why is Achariyar silent about that “work”? Why should that dirty secret not be exposed?

Did Krishna mean that “remaining in service” consists of doing no labour and conducting a business based upon the ignorance of working people?

Achariyar says the story teaches us to labour for humankind's welfare. Do Brahmins—his own community, honoured by the very Krishna of the Gita with the claim “Brahmins are my gods”—labour for humankind? They have devised a cunning occupational system under which they need not labour even for their own livelihood.

If Achariyar explains the secret of Aryan life alongside the secret of the story, it would be useful. Will he?

If they truly possess a desire to serve human life, would these people of comfort use the whip called scripture to drive the working people whose labour feeds and protects them, bending them like a bull under a nose-rope, restraining them like a horse under a bridle and making them toil like oil-press cattle?

According to Manu's law, a Brahmin must not farm. He must not eat coarse grains, but requires fine fragrant rice and royal luxury. He is supposedly unfit to join the army, yet demands an honorarium to “bless” it. Is this not the service of your community, Achariyar?

You see astonishing transformations across the world and workers awakening everywhere. Yet your community still seeks the same painless life it arranged when it wandered along the Ganges long ago. Is this what you call the magnificent principle of serving humankind? What answer will Achariyar give?

I do not know what “grace” will come to the Japanese emperor when he returns a year later to speak to his ancestral spirits. Looking at the present condition and balance of military strength, Japan's future appears surrounded by darkness; its rising sun will certainly set.

Japan's victories in the first year do not prove its strength. Many factors caused Britain's defeats. Japan will now discover the suffering caused by confronting strength. I have no doubt of it. Before then, Chittagong-like raids may occur; we must not lose heart. Chungking should be a lesson to us all. The Japanese rained destruction upon it. Buildings have become broken walls, yet the people's hearts remain unshaken and they continue fighting resolutely. They have built many caves in the mountain regions and reportedly use them as shelters during air raids.

Many thousands of Chinese have volunteered for air-raid-protection work. The Chinese bravely confronted the danger from aircraft. We must show that we are no less capable. If we remain firm for another year, then when the Japanese emperor goes to petition his ancestral spirits next year, he will sing his complaint in the plaintive Mukhari raga. This is the thought of the Islamic Dravidian.

Let Japanese and Aryans worship their respective great-grandfathers' spirits and say whatever they please. We must not fall into their deceitful nets.

Aryans ruined thought and turned our people into walking corpses. At times we ask why they did so and why they described so many obscene stories as religion. They know that the rubbish they call Veda, religion, scripture and ritual is obscene. Knowing it, they fed it to our people. They understood the secret: drain self-respect, indignation and courage from the race one wishes to defeat, and there will be no resistance; a people capable of opposition will instead obey sweetly. They applied that method skilfully. They fed Dravidians ideas that diminished their ability and courage, then lived and slept in comfort.

News says the Japanese now corrupt the people of countries caught in their grasp through the same method. The conquered must labour for them like oil-press cattle and never acquire the temperament to ask why. They must endure beatings and possess no self-respect; their very sense of dignity must fade. That is Japan's intention, which it tries to enforce through military rule.

Yet how long can people be kept suppressed? What if one day resentment drives them to rise? To make conquered people incapable of action, the Japanese reportedly distribute destructive intoxicants such as cannabis and opium free of charge. People who consume them become dependent and so crazed that they may choose death if denied the drug. Their bodies quickly deteriorate, while courage, dignity and indignation die within. They then remain forms that labour, not beings with feeling.

Japan is said to implement this destructive programme in occupied countries. The Japanese government has reportedly employed 1,500 scientists to manufacture various intoxicants in laboratories. A Japanese military manual is even said to prescribe that the superior Japanese race must not touch such substances; they are to be supplied only to enslaved Chinese and Indians.

When our people roll on the ground during Purattasi, shave their heads, pierce their bodies with needles for Kali or other spirits, lie beneath a rolling temple car, carry fire pots, walk upon flames, bear ritual frames or pierce their mouths with spears, Brahmins look on and pat them approvingly as “devout.” But they do not perform these acts themselves.

“Unfit for us, suitable for servants”—that is the real Aryan attitude towards most religious rites, scriptures, stories and ceremonies. Their plan is that those destined to be servants must regard such tricks as divine worship.

The Japanese likewise ruin other peoples with intoxicants. In the age of their great-grandfathers, our own great-grandfathers lost both authority and honour. In our age we must recover them. That is our Gita:

Dignity is greater than life!

People are greater than religion!

We demand freedom.

We are slaves to none, and none need be slaves to us.

These are our Gita—the path laid down by Periyar!

(27 December 1942)
